          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe as secondary prevention after a heart attack?",
          "answer": "**Seek emergency care now** if you are having chest pain, pressure, or other heart symptoms. Call emergency services or go to an emergency department right away. Do not wait to see whether it passes, and do not drive yourself. Nothing in your question describes an emergency, so this is a general precaution.\n\nBased on the guidance I could retrieve, secondary prevention is the set of measures for someone who already has established coronary artery disease, such as a previous heart attack, a procedure to open the arteries, or narrowing seen on a scan. The aim is to lower the chance of another heart event. The source is *Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis*, from major cardiology society consensus guidance (educational summary).\n\nThe guidance describes it as a package, not a single treatment. Its benefit comes from using the parts together. The components it names include:\n\n- **Antiplatelet therapy** (medicines that stop blood cells clumping). Most people take one long term. Some take two for a set period after a heart attack or stent, with the length based on bleeding risk.\n- **High-intensity statin therapy** (a class of cholesterol-lowering medicine).\n\nThe passage I received cut off after the statin point, so I cannot tell you what other components the guidance lists, and I cannot give doses. The guidance also notes that non-drug measures are often the ones that get dropped. If you want to know what applies to you, discuss it with your clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
